    <p>All three fuel types move in near-perfect synchronization with Brent Crude at the global aggregate level, confirming that international commodity prices are the dominant driver of retail fuel costs. However, as later sections reveal, national policy regimes create substantial deviations from this baseline.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 2: Temporal Trends -->
  <div class="section" id="temporal-trends">
    <h2>2. Temporal Price Trends & Volatility</h2>
    <p>The 2020–2026 period captures three distinct market regimes: the COVID-19 demand collapse, the post-pandemic recovery and 2022 supply crisis, and the elevated-price stabilization phase from 2023 onward.</p>

    <h3>2.1 Yearly Trajectory</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Year</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Brent (USD/bbl)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>2020</td><td>1.48</td><td>1.39</td><td>1.11</td><td>69.44</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2021</td><td>2.28</td><td>2.13</td><td>1.71</td><td>106.59</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2022</td><td>2.51</td><td>2.34</td><td>1.88</td><td>117.12</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2023</td><td>2.23</td><td>2.09</td><td>1.68</td><td>104.43</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2024</td><td>2.55</td><td>2.38</td><td>1.91</td><td>118.99</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2025</td><td>2.54</td><td>2.37</td><td>1.90</td><td>118.71</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2026</td><td>2.65</td><td>2.47</td><td>1.99</td><td>123.75</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>2.2 Shock Events</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="highlight-box">
        <strong>COVID-19 Demand Shock (Mar–Apr 2020)</strong><br>
        Brent Crude fell from ~$71 to $47.97/bbl (−32%). Global average petrol prices dropped <strong>33.0%</strong>, from $1.53/L to $1.02/L. The pass-through was immediate and symmetric, with retail prices tracking crude declines within weeks.
      </div>
      <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>2022 Energy Crisis (Feb–Mar 2022)</strong><br>
        Brent spiked to an all-time high of <strong>$130.00/bbl</strong> on March 28, 2022. Global average petrol reached <strong>$2.63/L</strong> during the crisis window. Unlike the COVID collapse, the upward shock was partially absorbed in high-subsidy regimes (see Section 4).
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>2.3 Brent-to-Retail Correlation</h3>
    <p>At the global weekly aggregate level, the Pearson correlation between Brent Crude and retail prices is exceptionally strong:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brent ↔ Petrol:</strong> r = 1.000</li>
      <li><strong>Brent ↔ Diesel:</strong> r = 1.000</li>
      <li><strong>Brent ↔ LPG:</strong> r = 1.000</li>
    </ul>
    <p>At the individual-country level, correlations remain high for most markets (median r ≈ 0.999), but diverge sharply in heavily subsidized or politically managed economies. Venezuela (r = 0.081), Iran (r = 0.336), and Libya (r = 0.472) show the weakest pass-through, indicating effective (if costly) price isolation from global markets.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['pass_through'] + """" alt="Pass-through analysis">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 2. Weekly percentage change in Brent Crude vs. global average petrol price, segmented by subsidy regime. Low-subsidy countries exhibit near-perfect pass-through (r = 0.996), while high-subsidy countries show dampened responsiveness (r = 0.712).</div>
    </div>

    <p>Weekly volatility (standard deviation of percentage changes) is <strong>1.55%</strong> for petrol in low-subsidy markets, closely mirroring Brent's <strong>1.54%</strong>. In high-subsidy markets, petrol volatility is paradoxically higher at <strong>2.18%</strong>, driven by discrete policy adjustments rather than smooth market tracking.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 3: Regional Comparison -->
  <div class="section" id="regional-comparison">
    <h2>3. Regional Price Disparities</h2>
    <p>Fuel prices are not evenly distributed globally. Europe and Oceania consistently record the highest retail prices, while the Middle East and South America sit at the opposite extreme. This spread reflects a combination of taxation policy, subsidy regimes, and purchasing-power considerations.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['regional_comparison'] + """" alt="Regional comparison">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 3. Average fuel prices by region (2020–2026). Europe leads in all three fuel categories; the Middle East records the lowest average prices.</div>
    </div>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Region</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th>Tax %</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Europe</td><td>3.70</td><td>3.52</td><td>2.78</td><td>34.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Oceania</td><td>3.66</td><td>3.47</td><td>2.74</td><td>38.7%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Asia</td><td>2.14</td><td>1.98</td><td>1.61</td><td>23.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>North America</td><td>2.06</td><td>1.89</td><td>1.55</td><td>29.2%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Africa</td><td>1.55</td><td>1.43</td><td>1.16</td><td>25.8%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>South America</td><td>1.43</td><td>1.29</td><td>1.07</td><td>18.2%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Middle East</td><td>1.23</td><td>1.14</td><td>0.92</td><td>19.6%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>The <strong>regional price spread</strong> — the gap between the highest- and lowest-priced regions — widened from <strong>$1.61/L</strong> in 2020 to <strong>$2.87/L</strong> in 2026. This divergence is driven primarily by Europe's elevated tax burden (34.6% average) versus the Middle East's heavy subsidy regime, which holds average petrol prices below $1.25/L despite rising crude costs.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['regional_trajectories'] + """" alt="Regional trajectories">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 4. Regional petrol price trajectories by year. Europe and Oceania maintain a persistent premium; Middle Eastern prices remain flat and subsidized.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['regional_spread'] + """" alt="Regional spread">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 5. Regional petrol price spread (max – min regional average) by year. The gap has grown steadily, reaching $2.87/L in 2026.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 4: Subsidy & Tax Analysis -->
  <div class="section" id="subsidy-tax-analysis">
    <h2>4. Impact of Subsidies and Taxation on Retail Prices</h2>
    <p>Government intervention is the single most powerful determinant of retail fuel prices after the crude oil benchmark itself. This analysis segments countries into <em>High Subsidy</em> (High + Very High) and <em>Low Subsidy</em> (Low + Medium) regimes.</p>

    <h3>4.1 Subsidy Price Dampening Effect</h3>
    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">84.5%</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Subsidy Dampening (Petrol)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">$0.45</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">High-Subsidy Avg Petrol</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">$2.93</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Low-Subsidy Avg Petrol</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">15.0%</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Avg Tax (High Subsidy)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">31.0%</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Avg Tax (Low Subsidy)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>High-subsidy countries charge consumers an average of just <strong>$0.45/L</strong> for petrol — a <strong>84.5% discount</strong> relative to low-subsidy markets ($2.93/L). This dampening is consistent across diesel (84.9%) and LPG (84.5%), indicating that subsidy design is broadly fuel-neutral in its protection.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['subsidy_impact'] + """" alt="Subsidy impact">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 6. Average fuel prices by subsidy level. High-subsidy regimes hold prices at roughly one-sixth of low-subsidy levels.</div>
    </div>

    <h3>4.2 Pass-Through Differences</h3>
    <p>Subsidies do more than lower price levels — they alter the <em>dynamics</em> of price transmission:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Low-subsidy markets:</strong> Weekly Brent-to-petrol change correlation = <strong>0.996</strong>. These markets function as efficient pass-through mechanisms.</li>
      <li><strong>High-subsidy markets:</strong> Weekly change correlation drops to <strong>0.712</strong>. Governments actively smooth or delay crude price movements.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>However, this stabilization comes with a cost: high-subsidy markets exhibit <strong>higher measured volatility (2.18% weekly std. dev.)</strong> than low-subsidy markets (1.55%), because administered prices adjust in discrete, politically timed steps rather than tracking continuous market movements.</p>

    <h3>4.3 Subsidy Distribution by Income Level</h3>
    <p>Subsidies are not concentrated in low-income countries. In fact, high-income nations have the lowest share of high-subsidy regimes (12.5%), while middle-income and low-income countries show similar rates (~34–35%). This pattern reflects the concentration of oil-producing Gulf states (high-income, high-subsidy) and the fiscal constraints facing many developing economies.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['subsidy_income'] + """" alt="Subsidy by income">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 7. Left: Petrol prices by national income level and subsidy regime. Right: Tax burden by income level and subsidy regime. High-income, low-subsidy countries combine high prices with high taxes.</div>
    </div>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Subsidy Regime</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax (%)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>4.00</td><td>37.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High</td><td>High</td><td>0.40</td><td>14.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Middle</td><td>Low</td><td>2.15</td><td>24.4%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Middle</td><td>High</td><td>0.54</td><td>14.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Low</td><td>Low</td><td>1.93</td><td>27.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Low</td><td>High</td><td>0.38</td><td>15.1%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 5: Income Level -->
  <div class="section" id="income-level">
    <h2>5. Fuel Affordability by Income Level</h2>
    <p>Segmenting countries by GNI per capita classification reveals a paradox: high-income nations charge the highest retail prices, but this reflects heavy taxation rather than unaffordability. Low- and middle-income countries face lower nominal prices, yet these represent a larger share of household expenditure.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['income_comparison'] + """" alt="Income comparison">
      <div class="chart-caption">Figure 8. Left: Fuel prices by national income level. Right: Average tax percentage by income level. High-income countries impose the heaviest tax burden (34.7% on average).</div>
    </div>

    <h3>5.1 Price Levels and Tax Burden</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax (%)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>High</td><td>3.55</td><td>3.35</td><td>2.66</td><td>34.7%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Middle</td><td>1.59</td><td>1.47</td><td>1.20</td><td>21.1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Low</td><td>1.39</td><td>1.28</td><td>1.04</td><td>22.9%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>5.2 Energy Equity Considerations</h3>
    <p>Low-income countries impose a tax burden (22.9%) comparable to middle-income countries (21.1%), despite lower average incomes. This suggests that fuel taxation in the poorest nations is already near a fiscal ceiling — further increases risk political resistance and demand destruction.</p>

    <p>Price stability, measured by the coefficient of variation (CV) of weekly petrol prices, is uniform across income levels (CV ≈ 0.178) because global crude movements dominate local dynamics. The real difference lies in <em>who bears the risk</em>: in high-income, low-subsidy countries, consumers face market prices directly; in low-income, high-subsidy countries, governments absorb price volatility through fiscal outlays.</p>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Equity Insight:</strong> The cheapest fuel in absolute terms is found in subsidized low-income markets (e.g., Libya, Venezuela, Nigeria), but these are often the countries least able to sustain fiscal transfers. The subsidy burden falls on government budgets that could otherwise fund health, education, or infrastructure.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 6: Key Findings & Policy Implications -->
  <div class="section" id="key-findings">
    <h2>6. Key Findings & Policy Implications</h2>
    <p>The following findings synthesize the quantitative evidence into actionable insights for energy policymakers.</p>

    <ol class="findings-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Brent Crude is the dominant price driver, but policy creates massive divergence.</strong><br>
        Global aggregate correlations between Brent and retail prices approach 1.000, yet the gap between the most expensive region (Europe, $3.70/L) and the cheapest (Middle East, $1.23/L) has widened to $2.87/L. <em>Implication:</em> Policymakers cannot control crude prices, but they have substantial levers — taxation and subsidy design — to determine where their country sits on the global price distribution.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Subsidies are highly effective at dampening prices — and highly expensive.</strong><br>
        High-subsidy regimes reduce average petrol prices by <strong>84.5%</strong> relative to low-subsidy markets. However, weekly price volatility is actually <em>higher</em> in subsidized markets (2.18% vs. 1.55%) due to discrete administrative adjustments. <em>Implication:</em> Consider targeted cash transfers or price-smoothing funds instead of blanket price controls. These preserve consumer protection while reducing fiscal exposure and market distortion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Low-subsidy markets offer near-perfect crude price pass-through.</strong><br>
        Weekly Brent-to-retail change correlations reach 0.996 in low-subsidy regimes. This efficiency supports market-based allocation but exposes consumers to full volatility. <em>Implication:</em> In deregulated markets, introduce automatic stabilizers (e.g., variable excise taxes that rise when crude falls and fall when crude spikes) to buffer consumers without destroying price signals.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>High-income countries tax fuel heavily; low-income countries subsidize it heavily.</strong><br>
        High-income nations average 34.7% fuel taxes, while 34.8% of low-income country observations fall under high-subsidy regimes. This creates a regressive global pattern where the poorest governments bear the largest fiscal burden to keep prices low. <em>Implication:</em> Low-income countries should prioritize subsidy reform paired with social safety nets. International financial institutions can support this transition with bridge financing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The regional price spread is widening — and will continue to widen without coordination.</strong><br>
